WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.640
Every observation is incomplete.

00:01.980 --> 00:06.660
It is the absolute, non-negotiable rule of engaging with the physical world.

00:07.020 --> 00:10.480
And yet, I mean, we spend our entire lives pretending it isn't true.

00:10.660 --> 00:11.680
We really do.

00:12.060 --> 00:13.420
When you hear a statement like,

00:13.760 --> 00:15.200
every observation is incomplete,

00:15.920 --> 00:17.560
it almost just washes over you.

00:17.640 --> 00:18.420
It sounds familiar, right?

00:18.420 --> 00:19.820
Right. It feels like common sense.

00:20.000 --> 00:20.420
Exactly.

00:21.060 --> 00:24.220
You, the listener, you think about a photograph of your family,

00:24.480 --> 00:27.000
or a hazy memory of your childhood bedroom,

00:27.000 --> 00:29.680
or even just looking at a GPS map on your phone.

00:30.000 --> 00:33.300
We all fundamentally understand that these things leave details out.

00:33.500 --> 00:35.440
Yeah, the map on your phone doesn't show you

00:35.440 --> 00:38.040
the individual blades of grass on the side of the highway.

00:38.180 --> 00:38.480
Right.

00:38.820 --> 00:40.920
But I want you, the listener, to pause for a second.

00:41.680 --> 00:46.660
Try to frame this not as some sort of technological flaw in our tools

00:46.660 --> 00:47.800
or a bug in our biology,

00:48.080 --> 00:50.880
but as a profound, almost unsettling mystery.

00:51.520 --> 00:54.020
If every single observation leaves something out,

00:54.360 --> 00:56.000
how do you actually know anything at all?

00:56.000 --> 00:57.340
That is the core of it.

00:57.340 --> 01:03.660
The problem is that we intuitively treat observation as a transparent window.

01:03.980 --> 01:07.300
We feel like we are looking through our eyes or through a camera lens

01:07.300 --> 01:09.560
or through a microscope directly at base reality.

01:09.740 --> 01:11.040
Like the glass is perfectly clear.

01:11.120 --> 01:11.580
Exactly.

01:12.120 --> 01:14.980
But the moment you accept the mathematical reality

01:14.980 --> 01:16.840
that every observation is incomplete,

01:16.840 --> 01:20.300
you have to admit that the window isn't transparent at all.

01:20.360 --> 01:20.900
It's a filter.

01:20.900 --> 01:25.280
And that filter is actively, violently destroying information

01:25.280 --> 01:27.480
before it ever reaches your brain.

01:27.780 --> 01:30.020
And what happens to that lost information of the universe?

01:30.600 --> 01:33.500
How do we take the fractured, blurry fragments

01:33.500 --> 01:35.180
that actually survive the filter

01:35.180 --> 01:37.620
and use them to build the seamless reality

01:37.620 --> 01:39.120
we feel like we live in every day?

01:39.220 --> 01:40.740
That is exactly what we're going to unpack.

01:40.900 --> 01:41.160
Right.

01:41.160 --> 01:44.900
And that is the core mission of our deep dive into the source material today.

01:44.900 --> 01:48.260
We are looking at a massive independent research draft

01:48.260 --> 01:54.680
titled Holonomic Space, Field Theory, Mariology, and the Geometry of Admissibility.

01:55.160 --> 01:56.580
It's by an author named Flixian.

01:56.920 --> 01:58.940
And the scope of this work is just staggering.

01:59.460 --> 02:01.100
Staggering is honestly the right word.

02:01.600 --> 02:05.640
We are going to be tracking a single unifying mathematical framework

02:05.640 --> 02:08.140
across incredibly diverse disciplines.

02:08.140 --> 02:10.860
We're talking about the rules of cartography,

02:11.340 --> 02:13.300
the cognitive psychology of human memory,

02:13.680 --> 02:18.040
the literal vector mathematics of how artificial intelligence maps language,

02:18.280 --> 02:22.200
all the way out to cosmology and the physical horizons of the universe.

02:22.400 --> 02:23.360
Which, I have to admit,

02:23.820 --> 02:26.740
sent my skepticism radar into absolute overdrive

02:26.740 --> 02:28.640
when I first looked at the stack of sources.

02:28.820 --> 02:29.840
Oh, I'm sure it did.

02:30.020 --> 02:33.320
Because usually when you see a paper that claims to connect the map

02:33.320 --> 02:36.780
of the London Underground to the causal horizons of a black hole,

02:36.780 --> 02:38.780
you assume you're reading science fiction,

02:39.120 --> 02:41.420
or at least some very loose philosophical poetry.

02:41.580 --> 02:41.680
Right.

02:41.860 --> 02:43.760
The crackpot theory alarm goes off.

02:43.840 --> 02:44.260
Exactly.

02:44.700 --> 02:47.060
But the rigor of this text is what kept me reading.

02:47.740 --> 02:50.720
Flixian is strictly intellectually honest, almost to a fault.

02:51.080 --> 02:54.540
They categorize every single claim in the text into four distinct levels.

02:54.720 --> 02:54.960
Yes.

02:55.360 --> 02:59.240
Proven, established framework, conjectured, and open problem.

02:59.380 --> 02:59.820
Exactly.

03:00.220 --> 03:03.300
So we always know exactly where the math is rock solid

03:03.300 --> 03:06.180
and where we are, you know, standing on the edge of the unknown.

03:06.180 --> 03:08.540
That structural honesty is so rare,

03:09.000 --> 03:12.460
especially in theoretical physics and complex systems theory.

03:13.240 --> 03:16.360
It's dangerously easy to present a beautiful conjecture

03:16.360 --> 03:19.220
as an established fact just because the math is elegant.

03:19.780 --> 03:22.320
The author resists that urge completely,

03:22.520 --> 03:24.760
which gives the framework so much more weight.

03:24.980 --> 03:28.840
So our mission is to unpack the central thesis of Flixian's text.

03:29.460 --> 03:30.700
And the thesis is this.

03:31.620 --> 03:33.240
Observation is not access.

03:33.240 --> 03:36.060
Observation is admissible projection.

03:36.480 --> 03:38.300
That phrase is going to come up a lot.

03:38.380 --> 03:38.980
It really is.

03:39.060 --> 03:41.640
We are going to figure out the mechanics behind those words.

03:41.840 --> 03:44.920
We need to understand how the universe hides its secrets from us

03:44.920 --> 03:47.520
and the incredible hidden machinery that we

03:47.520 --> 03:49.420
and the artificial minds we are building

03:49.420 --> 03:53.520
use to construct a worldview out of the pieces we are allowed to keep.

03:53.520 --> 03:55.860
And a really great entry point into this

03:55.860 --> 03:58.660
and where the text starts its mathematical grounding

03:58.660 --> 04:01.840
is the most intuitive example of a projection we have, the map.

04:02.040 --> 04:02.220
Right.

04:02.280 --> 04:03.980
The classic Alfred Krasivsky quote,

04:04.080 --> 04:05.500
the map is not the territory,

04:05.940 --> 04:08.040
which on its face feels like common sense.

04:08.100 --> 04:10.820
You don't try to fold up the actual physical city of New York

04:10.820 --> 04:11.940
and stuff it in your glove box.

04:11.960 --> 04:13.660
We need a very big glove box.

04:13.920 --> 04:14.140
Right.

04:14.300 --> 04:16.040
You use a piece of paper or a screen.

04:16.040 --> 04:20.360
But the text pushes us to look at why that map is useful.

04:21.020 --> 04:24.060
A map is functional precisely because it is wrong.

04:24.660 --> 04:26.220
It is defined by its omissions.

04:26.880 --> 04:30.300
If we tried to create a map that didn't leave any information out,

04:30.560 --> 04:32.280
what would we actually have?

04:32.680 --> 04:35.360
You have a one-to-one scale replica of reality

04:35.360 --> 04:37.860
to step out of your front door, unfold your map,

04:37.900 --> 04:39.640
and it would just cover your entire neighborhood.

04:39.940 --> 04:42.080
It would be utterly useless for navigation.

04:42.400 --> 04:44.280
Because navigating requires compression.

04:44.280 --> 04:47.920
A successful map has to ignore massive amounts of reality

04:47.920 --> 04:50.100
to highlight a specific relationship.

04:50.760 --> 04:54.500
A road map ignores elevation so you can see the highways clearly.

04:55.060 --> 04:57.620
A topographic map ignores political borders.

04:57.920 --> 05:00.040
A subway map is a fantastic example, too.

05:00.280 --> 05:03.460
It completely distorts physical geographical distance

05:03.460 --> 05:06.440
in order to preserve the topological sequence of the stops.

05:06.540 --> 05:06.900
Exactly.

05:07.440 --> 05:09.600
In the mathematical language Fliction uses,

05:09.880 --> 05:13.060
which falls under the established framework category in the text, by the way,

05:13.060 --> 05:14.280
the map is a projection.

05:14.640 --> 05:18.340
It's a strict mathematical operation that preserves a very specific target structure

05:18.340 --> 05:20.400
while deliberately annihilating the rest.

05:20.920 --> 05:22.560
And the stuff that gets annihilated.

05:22.780 --> 05:24.000
The text gives that a name, too.

05:24.080 --> 05:24.740
It's called the kernel.

05:25.340 --> 05:29.120
And I have a feeling this word is going to haunt us for the rest of this deep dive.

05:29.240 --> 05:30.360
Oh, it absolutely will.

05:30.780 --> 05:36.660
The kernel is the mathematical graveyard of all the details the map fails to distinguish.

05:36.660 --> 05:38.760
Think about a topographic map.

05:39.340 --> 05:41.460
If you have two different cities,

05:42.040 --> 05:45.540
and they happen to sit at the exact same elevation above sea level,

05:45.840 --> 05:48.540
the elevation map cannot tell them apart.

05:48.920 --> 05:49.240
Right.

05:49.380 --> 05:52.100
From the strict perspective of that specific map,

05:52.240 --> 05:53.860
those two cities are identical.

05:54.100 --> 05:54.480
Exactly.

05:54.820 --> 05:57.020
Their distinct identities have fallen into the kernel.

05:57.200 --> 06:00.960
And when two distinct realities are mapped to the exact same observation,

06:01.560 --> 06:04.140
mathematicians say they form an equivalence class.

06:04.140 --> 06:08.020
I was really trying to wrap my head around this equivalence class idea

06:08.020 --> 06:09.300
because it sounds so abstract.

06:09.700 --> 06:12.240
But the analogy I kept coming back to is sorting recycling.

06:12.400 --> 06:13.360
Oh, that's a good one.

06:13.540 --> 06:14.220
Walk me through it.

06:14.500 --> 06:16.060
Think about the physical mechanics of it.

06:16.580 --> 06:20.900
You've got a glass soda bottle, a plastic milk jug, and a cardboard cereal box.

06:21.760 --> 06:23.300
Sitting on your kitchen counter,

06:23.760 --> 06:26.260
they have completely unique complex identities.

06:26.640 --> 06:30.060
They have different chemical compositions, different labels, different physical histories.

06:30.680 --> 06:32.340
Maybe the bottle has a scratch on it.

06:32.340 --> 06:32.560
Sure.

06:32.560 --> 06:35.600
But the moment you throw them into the recycling bins,

06:36.320 --> 06:38.040
you are forcing them into a projection.

06:38.740 --> 06:41.180
Glass, plastic, paper.

06:41.540 --> 06:43.060
You've applied a rigid filter.

06:43.340 --> 06:43.580
Right.

06:43.940 --> 06:48.480
I've completely destroyed the distinct identity of the individual soda bottle.

06:49.060 --> 06:54.200
The recycling center doesn't care that it was a vintage cola bottle from 1998 with a scratch on the side.

06:54.600 --> 06:56.400
The sorting mechanism just sees glass.

06:56.600 --> 06:57.720
Everything else goes to the kernel.

06:57.860 --> 06:58.200
Exactly.

06:58.200 --> 07:03.600
All glass objects, no matter their shape or history, have been grouped into an equivalence class.

07:03.600 --> 07:09.440
The projection preserved the material composition, because that's what's admissible for the task of recycling.

07:09.440 --> 07:12.200
And the kernel swallowed every other detail.

07:12.200 --> 07:17.680
Let's take your recycling analogy and apply it to Solorzano's concept of the holonomic perspective.

07:18.160 --> 07:21.040
This is actually where fliction draws the name of the theory from.

07:21.660 --> 07:25.780
Usually when we think of the distance between two things, we think of physical space.

07:26.240 --> 07:28.260
Inches, miles, light years.

07:28.460 --> 07:29.480
Right. Geometric distance.

07:29.480 --> 07:33.640
But in a holonomic space, distance is not about geometry.

07:34.400 --> 07:37.700
It is about the cost of moving between these equivalence classes.

07:38.240 --> 07:41.600
It's the cost of admissible internal reconfigurations.

07:41.860 --> 07:46.180
Okay, break down the mechanics of a reconfiguration for me, because that sounds heavy.

07:46.500 --> 07:50.920
Well, within your recycling den, if you want to change a glass bottle into a glass jar,

07:51.260 --> 07:54.740
the cost is relatively low within the glass equivalence class.

07:54.860 --> 07:57.080
The system just melts the glass down and reshapes it.

07:57.080 --> 07:59.340
The material stays within the same holonomic state.

07:59.880 --> 08:03.220
But what if you want to change that glass bottle into a plastic jug?

08:03.380 --> 08:03.980
You can't.

08:04.300 --> 08:09.140
Not without some absurd alchemical process that completely breaks the rules of the sorting system.

08:09.420 --> 08:10.440
That is the whole point.

08:11.020 --> 08:15.600
Changing glass to plastic means crossing a fundamental boundary of the projection.

08:16.540 --> 08:20.960
So holonomic distance measures what it would take to transform one state into another

08:20.960 --> 08:24.220
without breaking the rules of the specific projection you're using.

08:24.680 --> 08:27.560
Two systems might look wildly different to our naked eye,

08:27.560 --> 08:31.280
but if they fall into the same equivalence class, holonomically speaking,

08:31.800 --> 08:32.940
they are right next to each other.

08:33.460 --> 08:36.500
That reframes how I think about similarity completely.

08:37.140 --> 08:41.160
Now, a map or a recycling bin obviously discards data.

08:41.460 --> 08:45.540
We inherently know the blue line on the paper isn't a literal rushing river.

08:45.540 --> 08:49.300
But the text argues that other projections are far more insidious

08:49.300 --> 08:51.800
because they trick us into thinking they are complete.

08:52.000 --> 08:52.520
Like a photograph.

08:52.900 --> 08:53.180
Yes.

08:53.720 --> 08:57.200
There was a brilliant quote from the photographer Richard Avedon included in the text.

08:57.740 --> 08:59.080
All photographs are accurate.

08:59.360 --> 09:00.440
None of them is the truth.

09:00.440 --> 09:02.120
It feels like the truth, though, doesn't it?

09:02.180 --> 09:02.800
It really does.

09:02.880 --> 09:05.700
You look at a photo and the visual geometry is right.

09:06.240 --> 09:07.600
The faces are recognizable.

09:08.120 --> 09:09.440
The proportions make sense.

09:09.800 --> 09:15.680
It feels like the truth because it preserves the specific structure our primate brains rely on most,

09:15.800 --> 09:17.800
which is 2D spatial arrangement.

09:17.800 --> 09:23.540
But, physically speaking, a camera is an incredibly violent projection device.

09:24.140 --> 09:27.700
It takes a vibrant, three-dimensional, dynamically evolving world

09:27.700 --> 09:33.020
and mathematically collapses it onto a flat, two-dimensional grid of pixels.

09:33.160 --> 09:34.580
It absolutely annihilates depth.

09:34.700 --> 09:37.180
You could have a giant mountain that is 50 miles away

09:37.180 --> 09:40.940
and a small, jagged pebble held three inches from the camera lens.

09:41.040 --> 09:41.240
Yeah.

09:41.320 --> 09:43.700
And on the flat image, they can look the exact same size.

09:43.780 --> 09:45.160
They fall into the same equivalence class.

09:45.160 --> 09:47.020
The loss of depth is huge, yes.

09:47.400 --> 09:51.420
But Fliction argues the much more profound loss is the destruction of time.

09:51.740 --> 09:53.700
This is a massive point in the framework.

09:54.260 --> 09:59.400
A photograph freezes an ongoing thermodynamic process into a single static state.

09:59.600 --> 10:02.380
It completely erases the causal history of the moment.

10:02.520 --> 10:06.480
Meaning the trajectory of how the system arrived at that state is just gone.

10:06.900 --> 10:08.380
Let's look at the mechanics of that.

10:08.660 --> 10:12.520
Imagine you take a high-resolution photograph of a brick wall

10:12.520 --> 10:15.340
with a massive, jagged crack running down the center.

10:15.780 --> 10:17.920
The photograph is undeniably accurate.

10:18.440 --> 10:21.640
You can measure the exact millimeters of the fissure on the image.

10:21.780 --> 10:21.960
Right.

10:22.160 --> 10:27.320
But looking solely at that image, you have absolutely no idea if that crack is 100 years old

10:27.320 --> 10:30.120
and the building has settled into structural stability

10:30.120 --> 10:33.360
or if that crack appeared 10 seconds ago

10:33.360 --> 10:36.560
and the entire wall is currently in the process of collapsing.

10:36.560 --> 10:40.300
The past and the future of the system have fallen into the kernel.

10:40.820 --> 10:40.980
Hold on.

10:41.040 --> 10:44.460
I want to play devil's advocate here because I think a lot of people would struggle with this.

10:45.140 --> 10:48.600
If I pull out my phone and take a picture of my dog sitting on the rug,

10:49.220 --> 10:50.740
I captured my dog.

10:51.060 --> 10:52.040
You can see his ears.

10:52.140 --> 10:52.780
You can see his fur.

10:53.080 --> 10:54.720
What do you mean I captured an equivalence class?

10:54.780 --> 10:56.280
It's not an abstract mathematical set.

10:56.320 --> 10:57.140
It's just my dog.

10:57.660 --> 10:59.680
Well, it's your brain telling you it's your dog.

11:00.220 --> 11:02.680
Mathematically, what your camera lens actually encountered

11:02.680 --> 11:05.300
is known in physics as an inverse problem.

11:05.880 --> 11:10.240
The image you captured, that specific arrangement of light and color on your screen,

11:10.740 --> 11:14.720
does not point backward to one single unique physical reality.

11:14.980 --> 11:18.520
It points backward to a massive space of compatible worlds.

11:18.900 --> 11:19.820
Compatible worlds?

11:20.040 --> 11:21.820
Wait, are we talking about multiverse theory here?

11:21.920 --> 11:23.180
No, no.

11:23.340 --> 11:27.280
We are staying firmly in this universe under the established framework label.

11:27.760 --> 11:30.480
A compatible world simply means a physical setup

11:30.480 --> 11:33.620
that could produce the exact same 2D projection.

11:34.280 --> 11:38.940
Yes, that image could have been produced by your actual dog sitting on the actual rug.

11:39.240 --> 11:43.220
But mechanically, that exact same pattern of photons hitting the camera sensor

11:43.220 --> 11:45.440
could have been produced by an incredibly realistic,

11:45.960 --> 11:48.180
hyper-detailed flat painting of your dog

11:48.180 --> 11:51.740
positioned at the exact right distance and angle in front of the lens.

11:51.740 --> 11:54.680
Okay, or like a really high-end holographic projection.

11:55.120 --> 11:56.540
Or a taxidermy dog.

11:56.540 --> 12:00.460
Or a complex arrangement of mirrors reflecting a dog from another room.

12:00.920 --> 12:02.440
The physical projection device,

12:02.560 --> 12:05.240
the camera cannot distinguish between any of those realities.

12:06.080 --> 12:08.480
From the perspective of the resulting image,

12:08.700 --> 12:12.040
the living, breathing dog and the perfectly angled flat painting

12:12.040 --> 12:14.740
belong to the exact same equivalence class.

12:15.240 --> 12:17.740
They yield the identical mathematical output.

12:18.000 --> 12:19.640
That is deeply weird to think about.

12:19.760 --> 12:20.300
It is.

12:20.300 --> 12:22.160
So when you look at the photo and say,

12:22.300 --> 12:23.220
that's my dog,

12:23.600 --> 12:26.020
you aren't just passively receiving information.

12:26.600 --> 12:30.760
Your brain is actively reconstructing the true world from the equivalence class.

12:31.260 --> 12:33.280
You are importing outside context,

12:33.400 --> 12:34.980
your memory that you own a dog,

12:35.320 --> 12:39.960
your knowledge that people don't usually keep hyper-realistic paintings of dogs on the floor

12:39.960 --> 12:44.400
to solve the inverse problem and declare the living dog as the reality.

12:44.400 --> 12:46.240
So observation is not access.

12:46.920 --> 12:48.820
Observation is admissible projection.

12:48.820 --> 12:52.700
The camera didn't give me direct access to the reality of the dog.

12:52.900 --> 12:54.860
It handed me a highly compressed projection.

12:55.360 --> 12:59.620
My brain had to do all the heavy lifting to rebuild the reality from the equivalence class.

12:59.880 --> 13:02.280
Which introduces a natural human impulse.

13:02.920 --> 13:07.940
If we know that maps and photos are losing information because they zoom out or flatten things down,

13:08.220 --> 13:09.400
our instinct is to say,

13:09.580 --> 13:10.740
well, the solution is obvious.

13:11.100 --> 13:12.220
We just need to look closer.

13:12.340 --> 13:13.160
We need to zoom in.

13:13.240 --> 13:13.460
Right.

13:13.580 --> 13:15.640
If the camera is losing the depth and the history,

13:16.240 --> 13:18.420
the logical fix is to get better equipment.

13:18.420 --> 13:19.860
Put the thing under a microscope.

13:20.240 --> 13:22.700
Get down to the cellular level or the atomic level.

13:22.960 --> 13:26.680
And then you finally strip away the ambiguity and see the truth of the object.

13:27.020 --> 13:31.080
And that is exactly where human intuition catastrophically fails us.

13:31.660 --> 13:34.700
The text introduces this as the microscope paradox.

13:35.380 --> 13:36.900
Fliction quotes William Blake here.

13:37.700 --> 13:39.840
To see a world in a grain of sand.

13:40.080 --> 13:41.400
And a heaven in a wildflower.

13:41.540 --> 13:42.560
It's one of my favorite poems.

13:42.560 --> 13:48.340
It's beautiful poetry, but fliction uses it as a warning about the physics of observation.

13:49.060 --> 13:52.160
Because magnification doesn't solve the projection problem.

13:52.540 --> 13:58.100
It actually fractures reality in a completely different but equally devastating way.

13:58.460 --> 14:00.880
When you zoom in, you destroy context.

14:01.100 --> 14:02.800
Let's walk through the mechanics of that.

14:03.080 --> 14:05.280
How does getting more detail destroy something?

14:05.380 --> 14:06.660
That feels really counterintuitive.

14:06.660 --> 14:10.060
Think about the biological architecture of a human heart.

14:10.400 --> 14:13.780
If you hold a piece of heart tissue and look at it with your naked eye,

14:14.380 --> 14:16.620
you are observing the macroscopic structure.

14:17.140 --> 14:21.120
You see the valves, the muscle fibers, the overall topology of the organ.

14:21.720 --> 14:26.300
Now, take that exact same tissue and put it under a powerful electron microscope.

14:26.700 --> 14:30.300
You are flooded with incredible high-resolution detail.

14:30.300 --> 14:35.740
You can see the individual cellular walls, the mitochondria powering the cells, the nucleus.

14:35.900 --> 14:37.580
But mechanically, what have you lost?

14:38.040 --> 14:40.580
Well, looking through the microscope, I can't tell it's a heart anymore.

14:40.720 --> 14:42.940
It just looks like a sprawling landscape of cells.

14:43.340 --> 14:44.600
The heartness of it is gone.

14:44.900 --> 14:47.220
The macrostructure has fallen into the kernel.

14:47.780 --> 14:48.840
Or consider metallurgy.

14:49.100 --> 14:54.060
If you take a beautifully forged steel sword and you zoom all the way into the atomic level,

14:54.340 --> 14:59.080
you can observe the gorgeous, intricate geometry of the iron and carbon crystal lattice.

14:59.080 --> 15:01.840
But you have completely lost the shape of the sword.

15:01.980 --> 15:05.440
The sharp edge, the hilt, the purpose of the object, all of it vanishes.

15:05.800 --> 15:09.020
This happens because reality is constructed of multiscale systems.

15:09.280 --> 15:11.480
Meaning reality isn't just happening at one level.

15:11.840 --> 15:17.140
There's meaningful, complex structure happening at the atomic scale and the cellular scale

15:17.140 --> 15:20.680
and the organ scale and the social scale all simultaneously.

15:21.020 --> 15:25.260
And this leads to one of the biggest aha moments in Fliction's entire framework.

15:25.900 --> 15:28.480
Information loss is bidirectional.

15:28.480 --> 15:29.600
Bidirectional loss.

15:29.660 --> 15:30.740
So it cuts both ways.

15:31.140 --> 15:33.960
We already prove that information is lost by coarse graining,

15:34.180 --> 15:37.560
by zooming out and blurring the details, like on the topographic map.

15:37.880 --> 15:41.520
But the microscope proves that information is also lost by overresolution.

15:42.220 --> 15:47.480
By zooming in too far, the broader context and the large-scale organization fall into the kernel.

15:47.740 --> 15:49.780
That implies a terrifying physical limit.

15:50.440 --> 15:53.960
It means that complete, total information about any complex system

15:53.960 --> 15:57.140
is mathematically inaccessible from any single scale.

15:57.520 --> 16:00.960
It is literally impossible to see the whole picture from one vantage point,

16:01.120 --> 16:03.300
no matter how advanced your technology gets.

16:03.300 --> 16:07.980
The text gives a fantastic, practical example from modern astronomy to illustrate this.

16:08.780 --> 16:12.520
If an astrophysicist wants to understand a distant, complex galaxy,

16:12.940 --> 16:16.120
they cannot just rely on one single mode of measurement.

16:16.460 --> 16:20.040
They need the spectral data to tell them the chemical composition of the stars.

16:20.640 --> 16:22.800
But that's one specific scale and projection.

16:23.300 --> 16:25.440
The spectrum tells them nothing about the shape.

16:25.440 --> 16:27.160
Right, it's just elemental lines.

16:27.500 --> 16:27.920
Exactly.

16:28.280 --> 16:31.220
So they need the optical image to show them the spatial structure,

16:31.320 --> 16:33.100
the spiral arms, the dust lanes.

16:33.240 --> 16:35.060
But the image is static,

16:35.400 --> 16:38.900
so they need the light curves over time to map out the temporal variation,

16:39.420 --> 16:42.200
like supernovas or pulsating variable stars.

16:42.960 --> 16:46.240
No single scale of observation gives you the galaxy.

16:46.800 --> 16:48.660
You are forced to reconstruct the object

16:48.660 --> 16:52.180
by patching together these different inherently incomplete projections.

16:52.180 --> 16:55.640
Okay, but if every single scale leaves something crucial out,

16:56.320 --> 16:58.600
how do we ever choose the right way to look at something?

16:59.500 --> 17:02.760
I mean, how do we avoid drowning in data that doesn't actually help us?

17:02.980 --> 17:04.560
That is the crucial question,

17:04.860 --> 17:09.020
and Fliction answers it with a principle called task-relative admissibility.

17:09.460 --> 17:14.440
The core argument, which Fliction labels as a proven theorem in information theory, by the way,

17:14.560 --> 17:18.600
is that useful information is not maximum information.

17:18.600 --> 17:24.080
The correct scale of observation is never the one with the highest sheer volume of data.

17:24.420 --> 17:28.020
It's the scale that is strictly admissible for your specific goal.

17:28.220 --> 17:29.560
I really love this concept.

17:29.700 --> 17:32.400
I was trying to map this onto something every day, and I thought about movies.

17:33.040 --> 17:34.400
Let's say I give you a task.

17:34.780 --> 17:38.700
I need you to write a comprehensive thematic review of the film, The Godfather.

17:39.240 --> 17:41.780
But instead of letting you sit in a theater and watch the movie,

17:42.160 --> 17:44.620
I hand you a hard drive containing a massive spreadsheet.

17:44.620 --> 17:51.000
This spreadsheet lists the exact, precise RGB color values of a single pixel dead in the center of the screen,

17:51.200 --> 17:55.720
and it updates that value 24 times a second for the entire three-hour runtime.

17:55.900 --> 17:56.920
That sounds like a nightmare.

17:57.220 --> 18:02.320
But from an information theory standpoint, you have handed me an enormous volume of highly precise data.

18:02.520 --> 18:06.120
It is perfectly accurate, maximally detailed, flawless data.

18:06.120 --> 18:11.320
But it is entirely, utterly inadmissible for the task of writing a film review.

18:11.760 --> 18:14.040
You cannot tell me if Marlon Brando's acting was good.

18:14.320 --> 18:17.000
You can't tell me about the pacing of the plot or the cinematography.

18:17.420 --> 18:18.760
The data is too fine.

18:19.140 --> 18:22.380
The narrative structure of the actual movie has fallen into the kernel.

18:22.500 --> 18:23.100
So true.

18:23.380 --> 18:28.000
To accomplish the task of writing the review, you actually need a lower resolution of data.

18:28.000 --> 18:36.300
You need to blur your eyes to the individual pixels and simply watch the coarse-grained, macroscopic shapes of the actors moving around the screen.

18:36.460 --> 18:39.020
That is a brilliant way to operationalize the math.

18:39.320 --> 18:43.400
It demonstrates perfectly why admissibility is a filter, not a threshold.

18:43.980 --> 18:47.940
We have this bias in the modern world that more data is automatically better,

18:48.340 --> 18:52.640
that if we just collect enough petabytes of information, the truth will magically emerge.

18:52.640 --> 18:58.360
But your RGB spreadsheet proves that data can be deeply inadmissible by being too granular.

18:58.920 --> 19:03.360
An observation is only mathematically admissible if it captures the structure relevant to the task,

19:03.680 --> 19:08.000
while successfully deliberately discarding the task-irrelevant structure into the kernel.

19:08.300 --> 19:12.860
So if all of our physical tools, the maps, the cameras, the microscopes, the telescopes,

19:12.860 --> 19:16.540
are all suffering from this bidirectional information loss,

19:16.740 --> 19:18.980
and they're all just casting mathematical shadows,

19:20.020 --> 19:21.560
what about the observer itself?

19:21.560 --> 19:24.360
How does the human mind handle this fractured data?

19:25.080 --> 19:29.560
This is where affliction transitions the framework from the external world to the internal world.

19:29.980 --> 19:33.760
If we look at human cognitive psychology through this holonomic lens,

19:34.240 --> 19:38.000
human memory is perhaps the ultimate example of admissible projection.

19:38.560 --> 19:41.740
The text opens this section with a haunting quote from Walter Benjamin.

19:42.520 --> 19:46.220
Memory is not an instrument for surveying the past, but its theater.

19:46.620 --> 19:49.800
Which perfectly encapsulates the findings of Frederick Bartlett,

19:49.800 --> 19:52.360
a pioneering psychologist from the 1930s.

19:53.320 --> 19:59.100
The established framework in cognitive science is that we are completely wrong about how our own memory feels.

19:59.640 --> 20:02.500
We intuitively believe memory is a recording device.

20:02.920 --> 20:06.400
You experience an event, your brain hits record and saves a video file.

20:06.820 --> 20:09.620
When you want to remember it, you hit play and watch the video back.

20:09.620 --> 20:14.140
I mean, that is undeniably how it feels when I have a really vivid memory of something.

20:14.280 --> 20:16.320
It feels like I'm just re-watching a mental movie.

20:16.540 --> 20:20.220
But the neuroscience and psychology prove that is a biological illusion.

20:21.220 --> 20:23.580
Memory is not a retrieval of a recording.

20:24.080 --> 20:26.500
It is an active, on-the-fly reconstruction.

20:26.500 --> 20:31.360
Fliction breaks this down into a two-step mathematical process happening in the brain.

20:32.060 --> 20:35.140
First, during the actual event, there is an encoding map.

20:35.780 --> 20:40.500
Your brain takes the massive, overwhelming, multi-scale sensory experience of reality

20:40.500 --> 20:45.260
and violently compresses it down into a few sparse neurological traces.

20:45.400 --> 20:46.260
Okay, that's step one.

20:46.340 --> 20:46.960
What's step two?

20:47.140 --> 20:50.200
Then, days or years later, there is a reconstruction map.

20:50.200 --> 20:56.520
Your brain takes those few sparse traces and essentially hallucinates a coherent, recalled event around them.

20:56.700 --> 20:59.080
Okay, so let's look at the mechanics of the encoding map.

20:59.260 --> 21:04.200
What gets preserved in those traces and what gets mercilessly thrown into the biological kernel.

21:04.380 --> 21:08.060
Think about the last time you went out to a really incredible dinner with friends.

21:08.320 --> 21:09.480
What do you actually remember?

21:09.940 --> 21:15.160
You preserve the narrative structure, who sat where, the general flow of the arguments or the jokes.

21:15.160 --> 21:20.320
You deeply preserve the emotional resonance, whether you felt relaxed, happy, or anxious.

21:20.680 --> 21:22.560
And you preserve the broad semantics.

21:23.100 --> 21:24.600
You know you ate Italian food.

21:24.780 --> 21:25.900
You know the wine was dry.

21:26.320 --> 21:27.680
But what falls into the kernel?

21:28.180 --> 21:30.660
Oh, the overwhelming majority of the physical reality.

21:30.880 --> 21:33.780
I have no idea exactly how many tables were in the room.

21:34.380 --> 21:38.040
I don't remember the exact Pantone color of the waiter's shirt.

21:38.320 --> 21:42.800
I couldn't tell you the ambient room temperature in exact degrees Fahrenheit.

21:42.800 --> 21:49.660
Exactly. The sensory precision, the raw numerical data, the exact geometry of the room, it is all annihilated.

21:50.040 --> 21:57.040
It falls into the kernel because your encoding map prioritizes emotional and semantic relevance over pixel-perfect accuracy.

21:57.680 --> 22:04.660
Your memory of the dinner is not mathematically exact, but it is highly admissible for the task of living your life and maintaining friendships.

22:05.220 --> 22:08.320
You don't need the exact room temperature to know you love your friends.

22:08.320 --> 22:13.600
But what happens when that reconstruction map, the hallucination part, gets it wrong?

22:14.060 --> 22:16.520
Let me give you a real-world example that happened to me.

22:16.940 --> 22:22.740
The other day, I vividly remembered this incredibly specific fact about octopus intelligence.

22:23.260 --> 22:25.420
I was telling everyone at a gathering about it.

22:25.820 --> 22:27.200
And someone stopped me and asked,

22:27.460 --> 22:29.000
Oh, that's fascinating. Where did you read that?

22:29.880 --> 22:31.500
And my brain just blue-screened.

22:31.560 --> 22:32.740
Huh, I know that feeling.

22:32.740 --> 22:42.080
I knew the fact with 100% certainty, but I had absolutely zero memory of who told it to me or what book I read it in or if I saw it on a late-night documentary.

22:42.360 --> 22:47.300
In cognitive psychology, that is a well-documented phenomenon called source-monitoring failure.

22:47.940 --> 22:53.560
But under Fliction's mathematical framework, it has a beautiful, precise, structural explanation.

22:53.560 --> 23:02.020
When your brain ran the encoding map during the original learning event, the factual content, the semantics about the octopus, was preserved as admissible.

23:02.680 --> 23:06.940
But the source tag, the context of how you learned it, fell into the kernel.

23:07.640 --> 23:13.820
So the fact and the source were decoupled, and the projection just deemed the source irrelevant to the task of knowing the fact.

23:14.000 --> 23:15.480
That's the exact mechanism.

23:16.100 --> 23:17.960
Because think about the equivalence class.

23:17.960 --> 23:26.700
Two entirely different physical experiences, reading a fact alone in a quiet library, or hearing it yelled across a loud bar by a friend,

23:27.160 --> 23:30.720
ended up producing the exact same semantic memory trace in your brain.

23:31.280 --> 23:33.740
They were compressed into the same equivalence class.

23:33.880 --> 23:42.680
So when your reconstruction operator fired up at the party to play the memory back, it had the fact, but it encountered the inverse problem for the origin.

23:42.680 --> 23:49.240
It couldn't reconstruct the unique source because the data to differentiate the library from the bar was fundamentally gone.

23:49.480 --> 23:49.880
Precisely.

23:50.280 --> 23:55.080
It's slightly terrifying to realize how much our brains are just guessing based on compressed shadows.

23:55.300 --> 23:57.600
But the text doesn't stop at human biology.

23:57.900 --> 24:03.940
It takes this exact same mathematical structure of memory and applies it directly to the architecture of modern artificial intelligence.

24:04.300 --> 24:04.600
Yes.

24:04.940 --> 24:07.220
Specifically focusing on machine learning embeddings.

24:07.220 --> 24:14.440
This is where affliction bridges the gap between squishy human psychology and the hard, cold math of computation.

24:15.080 --> 24:28.000
In modern AI, an embedding is a way of mapping incredibly complex, high-dimensional objects like words, or entire images or paragraphs of text, into a lower-dimensional vector space.

24:28.200 --> 24:33.380
Okay, let's slow down and explain the mechanics of a vector space for the listener, because it sounds like heavy jargon.

24:33.380 --> 24:40.820
Sure. Think of a vector space as a vast, multidimensional grid, where an AI organizes concepts based on numbers.

24:41.540 --> 24:44.360
The AI doesn't read the letters K-I-N-G.

24:44.760 --> 24:53.940
It takes the concept of a king and runs it through an algorithm that spits out a long string of coordinates, placing the concept of a king at a specific mathematical location in this space.

24:54.120 --> 25:02.600
It is trying to do exactly what your brain's encoding map does, compress the complex reality of human language into a manageable mathematical trace.

25:02.600 --> 25:04.700
And proximity in that space matters, right?

25:05.040 --> 25:10.440
Like the coordinates for king should end up physically close to the coordinates for queen or monarch in this mathematical grid.

25:10.780 --> 25:11.260
Precisely.

25:11.680 --> 25:15.180
The distance between the coordinates represents semantic similarity.

25:15.680 --> 25:19.920
But here is the counterintuitive part, and it ties right back to the microscope paradox.

25:20.540 --> 25:24.560
You might logically assume that a higher-dimensional embedding is always superior.

25:24.560 --> 25:31.360
You might think, why map a word to a 300-dimensional coordinate space when I could map it to a 10,000-dimensional space?

25:31.860 --> 25:36.100
More dimensions means more numbers, which means I can capture more detail about the word.

25:36.380 --> 25:38.720
I mean, more detail has to be better for the AI, right?

25:39.060 --> 25:42.500
But remember, useful information is not maximum information.

25:43.060 --> 25:48.240
If you force the AI to embed language into a space that is too large, it doesn't just learn the meaning.

25:48.240 --> 25:52.780
It starts accidentally encoding what the text calls task-irrelevant variation.

25:53.400 --> 25:54.960
What does that actually look like in an AI?

25:55.140 --> 25:57.820
How does an AI encode task-irrelevant variation?

25:58.080 --> 25:59.420
Let's stick with the word embeddings.

25:59.900 --> 26:05.300
If your task is to build an AI that understands the semantic meaning of text, for example,

26:05.800 --> 26:09.240
you want it to know that king and monarch mean roughly the same thing.

26:09.620 --> 26:15.460
You must design an embedding projection that deliberately, violently discards spelling, etymology, and phonetics.

26:15.460 --> 26:22.080
You want the fact that king starts with a K and monarch starts with an M to fall entirely into the mathematical kernel.

26:22.460 --> 26:23.280
Oh, I see the trap.

26:23.700 --> 26:28.160
If the coordinate space is too large, it has enough room to start mapping the physical letters.

26:28.640 --> 26:37.060
And if it does that, it might decide that king is mathematically closer to kangaroo or kite just because they share the same starting phonetics out.

26:37.300 --> 26:38.540
You've hit the nail on the head.

26:39.100 --> 26:44.120
The kernel of a successful semantic word embedding is incredibly dense with spelling and phonetic data.

26:44.120 --> 26:49.860
It has to throw all of that away so that the reconstructed output is highly admissible for meaning alone.

26:50.440 --> 26:56.720
If the dimension is too high, the phonetic sound, which is task-irrelevant noise for semantic meaning,

26:57.160 --> 27:01.880
contaminates the space and ruins the AI's ability to cluster concepts logically.

27:02.300 --> 27:07.200
In AI, just like in mapmaking, compression isn't just about saving hard drive space.

27:07.560 --> 27:12.300
It is the active mechanism of revealing structure by hiding noise.

27:12.300 --> 27:19.440
So whether we are talking about a physical topographic map, your blurry biological memory of an Italian restaurant,

27:19.620 --> 27:26.240
or the billion-parameter vector space of an artificial intelligence model, the underlying mathematics are identical.

27:26.520 --> 27:31.560
It is all about designing the appropriate projection and deliberately letting the right things fall into the kernel.

27:31.560 --> 27:36.800
But up to this point in our deep dive, we have only been talking about observation tools that we design,

27:37.020 --> 27:40.580
or biological systems that evolved within us to help us survive.

27:40.960 --> 27:44.220
A camera, a map, a brain, an AI.

27:44.560 --> 27:48.720
But what happens when the physical universe itself acts as the projection mechanism?

27:49.260 --> 27:52.320
We are moving out of psychology and engineering and into hard physics.

27:52.680 --> 27:52.880
Right.

27:52.880 --> 27:57.680
Let's talk about detectors, because all of physics relies on physical instruments to tell us what the universe is doing,

27:57.960 --> 28:02.160
and I think we have a tendency to treat a scientific instrument as the ultimate objective window.

28:02.480 --> 28:07.560
You stick a standard mercury thermometer into a glass of water, the red line goes up to 70 degrees,

28:07.660 --> 28:11.100
and you think, ah, I have bypassed human bias.

28:11.440 --> 28:13.420
I have seen the objective truth of the water.

28:13.760 --> 28:17.160
But Flickshane emphasizes that a detector is never a transparent window.

28:17.780 --> 28:19.020
It is a physical interface.

28:19.020 --> 28:22.300
And mechanically, it operates as a two-stage projection.

28:22.820 --> 28:28.320
The first stage is the actual physical interaction between the target system and the detector material.

28:28.820 --> 28:34.180
The second stage is the transduction, where the result of that physical interaction is mathematically converted

28:34.180 --> 28:40.680
into a macroscopic, readable output, like a red line moving up a glass tube or a number on a digital screen.

28:40.680 --> 28:43.060
Let's apply that two-stage projection to the thermometer.

28:43.440 --> 28:47.140
What is actually happening at the molecular level and what is being thrown into the kernel.

28:47.140 --> 28:52.240
When you put the thermometer in the glass, it is not measuring the individual water molecules.

28:52.760 --> 28:58.780
It is measuring the average kinetic energy of billions of water molecules physically colliding with the glass bulb.

28:59.160 --> 29:00.900
That is the interaction projection.

29:01.480 --> 29:06.880
The thermometer is completely physically blind to the exact trajectory, the specific velocity,

29:07.160 --> 29:10.360
and the quantum state of every single individual water molecule.

29:10.820 --> 29:15.480
All of that microscopic chaotic complexity falls directly into the kernel.

29:15.480 --> 29:20.800
Because there are probably millions or billions of completely different microscopic arrangements

29:20.800 --> 29:26.320
of those water molecules bouncing around that would all result in the exact same average impact energy.

29:26.420 --> 29:26.820
Exactly.

29:27.300 --> 29:31.340
Which means the macroscopic readout of 70 degrees is not a unique reality.

29:31.700 --> 29:33.780
70 degrees is an equivalence class.

29:34.460 --> 29:35.240
That is wild.

29:35.420 --> 29:37.180
The temperature is an equivalence class.

29:37.540 --> 29:42.260
And the text takes this from simple thermodynamics all the way to the cutting edge of quantum physics,

29:42.700 --> 29:44.640
using the search for dark matter as an example.

29:44.640 --> 29:50.280
Yes, the text uses an axion detector as a prime case study of this physical limitation.

29:50.980 --> 29:54.340
Now, fliction properly categorizes this under the conjectured label,

29:54.600 --> 29:58.980
as axions are hypothetical particles we are searching for to explain dark matter.

29:59.180 --> 29:59.400
Right.

29:59.820 --> 30:05.120
An axion detector is a highly sensitive instrument trying to sense a dark sector particle field

30:05.120 --> 30:08.760
interacting with a standard electromagnetic field inside a cavity.

30:08.760 --> 30:13.460
But the crucial point is that the detector cannot observe the axion field directly.

30:14.100 --> 30:18.780
It can only observe the tiny secondary signal produced by the interaction after that signal

30:18.780 --> 30:21.180
has been amplified and digitized by the machinery.

30:21.880 --> 30:25.500
And what is the danger in how physicists interpret that digitized signal?

30:26.320 --> 30:30.640
The danger lies in a profound epistemological trap that fliction outlines.

30:31.200 --> 30:34.000
Observable simplicity does not equal ontological simplicity.

30:34.000 --> 30:35.180
Hold on, we need to unpack that.

30:35.300 --> 30:40.360
Ontological simplicity, meaning how simple the thing actually is in reality versus how simple

30:40.360 --> 30:41.200
it looks on the screen.

30:41.640 --> 30:42.140
Precisely.

30:42.640 --> 30:47.080
Let's say the axion detector spits out a signal, and on the scientist's computer screen,

30:47.500 --> 30:52.140
it looks like a beautiful, smooth, highly predictable Gaussian bell curve.

30:52.140 --> 30:58.920
The human impulse is to say, ah, the underlying axion field governing dark matter follows simple

30:58.920 --> 31:00.120
Gaussian rules.

31:00.300 --> 31:02.720
But mechanically, that detector is a projection.

31:03.280 --> 31:07.780
It is violently averaging over millions of unobservable field degrees of freedom.

31:07.960 --> 31:08.240
Yes.

31:08.480 --> 31:13.900
The underlying axion field could be wildly complex, highly chaotic, and completely non-Gaussian.

31:14.420 --> 31:19.320
But because the physical interaction projection averages it all out, the underlying chaos falls

31:19.320 --> 31:23.660
into the kernel, and the final macroscopic readout looks incredibly simple.

31:24.020 --> 31:27.900
So we might be looking deep into the quantum universe, seeing what we think are simple,

31:28.100 --> 31:32.480
elegant rules and congratulating ourselves for figuring it out, when in reality, we are

31:32.480 --> 31:34.920
just seeing the mathematical limitations of our own thermometers.

31:35.320 --> 31:39.960
The universe might be a raging, chaotic storm, and we are just looking at a blurry shadow on

31:39.960 --> 31:40.300
the wall.

31:40.300 --> 31:42.960
It is a profoundly humbling thought for the sciences.

31:42.960 --> 31:49.520
But the projection problem becomes absolute, inescapable law when we transition to the ultimate

31:49.520 --> 31:53.300
physical limit discussed in the text, causal horizons.

31:53.580 --> 31:58.520
When I read causal horizons, my mind immediately went to black holes, the event horizon, the point

31:58.520 --> 31:59.200
of no return.

31:59.640 --> 32:04.260
Black holes are the most famous example of a horizon, but fliction is talking about cosmology

32:04.260 --> 32:05.680
on a universal scale.

32:06.240 --> 32:09.400
Every single observer in the universe has a causal horizon.

32:09.400 --> 32:13.880
Because the speed of light is finite, it takes time for information to travel.

32:14.520 --> 32:20.260
And because the universe has a finite age, there is a literal physical boundary in space

32:20.260 --> 32:25.280
beyond which no light, no gravity, no signal of any kind could have possibly reached us

32:25.280 --> 32:25.500
yet.

32:25.680 --> 32:29.800
Meaning the entire rest of the universe, the infinite expanse of everything beyond that

32:29.800 --> 32:31.660
boundary, is the ultimate kernel.

32:31.880 --> 32:33.360
It's just, it's inaccessible.

32:33.480 --> 32:34.840
It is fundamentally inaccessible.

32:34.960 --> 32:36.780
The observer does not get to choose this horizon.

32:36.780 --> 32:39.600
The geometric structure of space-time itself imposes it.

32:40.040 --> 32:45.140
And this absolute limit is where fliction introduces a master concept that weaves everything

32:45.140 --> 32:46.740
together, the ocularum.

32:46.960 --> 32:47.840
The ocularum.

32:48.200 --> 32:52.580
It sounds like an ancient mystical artifact, but it's a rigorously defined mathematical

32:52.580 --> 32:53.680
space in the text.

32:53.920 --> 32:57.300
It is the defining structure of reality for any observer.

32:57.780 --> 33:00.460
An ocularum is not just the stuff you are looking at.

33:00.460 --> 33:06.620
It is the entire structured operational domain that makes observation physically possible.

33:07.160 --> 33:08.480
It consists of three things.

33:09.060 --> 33:12.000
The accessible physical region, what is inside your horizon.

33:12.500 --> 33:17.280
The admissible data you are capable of pulling from that region like light waves or sound waves.

33:17.500 --> 33:22.660
And the specific mathematical projection your tools or your brain are using to parse it.

33:22.660 --> 33:25.900
I was trying to visualize the ocularum, and I came up with an analogy.

33:26.520 --> 33:30.820
Imagine I am kidnapped and locked inside a small room in the exact center of a massive,

33:31.020 --> 33:31.720
sprawling mansion.

33:31.800 --> 33:32.520
Okay, paint the picture.

33:32.900 --> 33:35.740
The room has no windows, and the door is locked from the outside.

33:35.740 --> 33:41.380
I have a notepad, and my goal is to draw a complete architectural blueprint of the entire mansion.

33:41.980 --> 33:47.100
But the only sensory input I have to work with are the muffled sounds coming through the drywall.

33:47.600 --> 33:51.600
